{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Security deposit transfer duty and withholding penalties","requirement":"When rental property is sold, the new owner must obtain the tenant's security deposit plus accrued interest and is responsible for investing it and returning it. Willfully withholding a deposit from a tenant receiving government financial assistance is penalized.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential rental security deposits; tenants receiving State or federal financial assistance, including welfare or rental assistance, are protected from willful withholding.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 per offense for willfully withholding a deposit from an assisted tenant; unlawful use of deposit monies is a disorderly persons offense punishable by a fine of at least $200 or up to 30 days' imprisonment, or both.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21","quoted_span":"If the property is sold or transferred it is the duty of the new owner to obtain the security deposit, plus accrued interest on the tenant’s deposit, that was collected by the former owner.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act good cause requirement","requirement":"A residential landlord may not evict or fail to renew a written or oral lease without good cause. Each cause except nonpayment of rent must be described in a written notice, and a Notice to Quit, and sometimes a Notice to Cease, is required.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential dwelling units; the Anti-Eviction Act protects blameless tenants and applies to landlords of residential premises.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"No residential landlord may evict or fail to renew a lease, whether it is a written or an oral lease without good cause. The landlord must be able to prove in court that he has grounds for an eviction.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act rent increase notice and unconscionability limit","requirement":"A landlord may not raise rent mid-lease; the old lease must be terminated and a new lease offered at the higher rent after written notice. An increase must not be unconscionable and must comply with municipal rent ordinances.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential tenants under written or oral leases; increases must comply with municipal rent control ordinances where they exist.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"The increase in rent must not be unconscionable; it must not be so unreasonable as to shock the conscience of a fair and honest person and must comply with any municipal ordinances governing rent increases.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings 30-year rent control exemption","requirement":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings are exempt from any local rent control ordinance for 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","key_value":"30 years","coverage_conditions":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings; exempt from local rent control for 30 years after completion of construction.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":"construction_date","min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"newly constructed multiple dwellings"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-84.5","quoted_span":"newly constructed multiple dwellings shall be exempt from any local rent control ordinances for a period of 30 years following completion of construction of the building","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Conversion cost rent increase prohibition","requirement":"When a building is converted to condominium, cooperative, or fee simple ownership, rents may not be increased to cover costs resulting solely from the conversion; increases for new services or amenities are allowed.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All tenants in a building converted to condominium, cooperative, or fee simple ownership, whether or not eligible for protected tenancy as senior citizens or disabled persons.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"buildings converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership"},"exemptions":"Increases to cover the cost of new services or amenities are permitted.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31","quoted_span":"If a building is converted to a condominium or cooperative form of ownership, or to fee simple ownership of units, rents may not be increased to cos ts resulting solely from the conversion (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"No State rent control; municipal rent control authorized","requirement":"New Jersey has no State law establishing, governing or controlling rents. Municipalities may adopt rent control or rent leveling ordinances, enforced by locally created boards.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All residential rentals in the State; whether a unit is covered depends on the municipal rent control ordinance.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"municipal rent control varies by locality"},"exemptions":"Housing owned or subsidized by HUD, some HUD-insured unsubsidized developments, and NJHMFA-financed projects are not subject to municipal rent control.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":null,"quoted_span":"The State of New Jersey has no laws that establish, govern or control rents. Municipalities may pass an ordinance establishing rent control or rent leveling.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"screening_restrictions","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Law Against Discrimination housing and source-of-income protection","requirement":"A landlord or rental agency may not refuse to rent or discriminate in the rental of housing, including on the basis of source of lawful income.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All landlord-tenant relationships, except two-family owner-occupied dwellings, rooms in an owner- or resident-occupied single home, and residences planned for and occupied by one sex as to familial status.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"all landlord-tenant relationships; exclusions for owner-occupied two-family dwellings and rooms in owner-occupied single homes"},"exemptions":"Two-family owner-occupied dwellings; rooms in an owner- or resident-occupied single home; residences planned exclusively for and occupied by one sex (YMCA, age-restricted housing) as to familial status.","penalty":"Monetary damages and possible order to rent the unit to the complainant; LAD fines up to $10,000 for a first offense.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 10:5-12","quoted_span":"requires equal treatment in the sale or rental of housing regardless of race, creed, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, marital status, civil union status, domestic partnership status, familial status, affectional or sexual orientation, gender  identity or expression, mental and physical disability, nationality, or source of lawful income.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
